2. Do the four scenarios boot after the fault?

  Scenario 1, corrupted package: three boots, BUT all three boot an image the
  installer never opened. cmd_install() verifies the manifest, the payload
  digests, the key and the bootchain hashes before Fat16Image(image); a
  corrupted package is refused before the disk is touched. So these three boots
  are three boots of an unchanged copy of build/nova.hdd. They prove the
  factory image boots, not that an interrupted install recovers.

  Scenario 2, USB removed mid-install: NOTHING BOOTS A USB REMOVAL. The only
  USB-removal evidence is four Python unit tests (TestUsbRemovalRecovery) that
  run the installer with an ordinary power-loss fault point and assert FAT
  structure: they never start QEMU. No code models a device disappearing: there
  is no EIO, no short read, no failed write, only os._exit(137). The shell stage
  that carries the "USB removal" label (stage 4 header) is the same 15
  power-loss states. The author's claim "(2) USB removal mid-install leaves the
  active slot intact and bootable under QEMU" is supported by power-loss boots
  of identical states, not by any USB-specific test.

  Scenario 3, power loss: real. 13 `before-<op>` points plus 2 `mid-` points =
  15 states, each booted under QEMU, plus one extra OVMF boot of the split
  commit. This is the part of D4 that is honestly what it says it is.

  Scenario 4, failed model load: no boot, by design (the contract asks for an
  honest report that no in-kernel model-load path exists). The report is
  printed, and two unit tests back it. Acceptable, and it is not claimed to be
  a boot test.

3. PLANT A (mine): delete the second commit of the atomic swap.
   In the scratch clone the UEFI twin commit is removed:

     inst.fault_before("commit_efi_ptr")
     # REVIEW PLANT A: skip the second commit (UEFI twin).
     pass

   This is an atomicity break: /limine.conf moves to the new release and
   /EFI/BOOT/LIMINE.CONF never does, so a UEFI machine keeps booting the old
   release forever, and the "atomic swap" is no longer atomic.

  $ bash scripts/test-recovery.sh
  power loss sweep: 15 states tested, 0 failures
  SABOTAGE GATE PASS (sabotage:broken-atomic-swap/SeaBIOS): mutant image refused to boot
  d4-recovery oracle: PASS
  suite rc=0

   Every check still passes. To show what the image really is, I installed the
   suite's own fixture package into a copy of build/nova.hdd with the planted
   installer and parsed both config chains:

  $ py -3 tools/atomic_install.py install --image build/d4/plantA.hdd \
      --manifest build/d4/manifest-good.json --payload-dir build/d4/payload-d4 \
      --trust-key build/d4/test-key.pub --state-file build/d4/plantA-state.json
  atomic_install: committed release 0.2.0-d4r1 (sequence 1) to slot A:
    config chain 759, kernel /NUCLEUS.A (216870 B, ...)
  install rc=0

  root /limine.conf  -> sequence 1 slot A
    path boot(1):/NUCLEUS.A#9ca02b43...
  EFI twin            -> "# Nucleus boot menu entry (Limine v12.9.x config format)."
    path boot(1):/NUCLEUS#11acccbd...        <- still the factory kernel

   Control, same fixture, unplanted installer (tools/atomic_install.py from
   bf44506), on a fresh copy of the same image:

  root /limine.conf -> sequence 1 | path boot(1):/NUCLEUS.A#9ca02b431
  EFI twin          -> sequence 1 | path boot(1):/NUCLEUS.A#9ca02b431

   So the plant is the only difference, and the suite cannot see it. There is
   no test anywhere that boots a *completed* install on either firmware: stage 3
   only does failed installs, and the sweep only does interrupted ones. The one
   OVMF power-loss boot is the split state, which is supposed to boot the old
   release, so it cannot notice either.

