   tools/atomic_install.py. Nothing asserts that the sweep ran 15 states, so a
   probe failure silently deletes 15 of the 19 boots and prints "0 failures".

6. Close read of one test: what is on each side, where did each come from.

   tests/test_recovery.py::TestPowerLossRecovery::test_power_loss_interruption_at_all_ops
   Left:  `r.returncode` and `r.stdout` from subprocess running
          tools/atomic_install.py with --fault-point <spec>.
   Right: the literal 137, the string "C3-FAULT", and later
          assert_image_bootable_and_safe() -> ai.verify_boot_target().
   Provenance: 137 and "C3-FAULT" come from Installer.fault_before() in the same
   module under test (os._exit(137), print "C3-FAULT ..."). That is the author's
   own code asserting its own exit code: it proves the fault hook fired, nothing
   more. verify_boot_target() is also the module under test, checking the module's
   own output. This test is a same-code self-check, and its value is only that
   the fault points exist in OPS and match the strings the harness passes; it
   cannot fail because the disk is atomic. The only external oracle in D4 is
   QEMU, and this test never starts it. That is why plant A is invisible: the
   tests that are independent of the installer are the boots, and they check one
   string.

