FINDINGS

F1 (blocking). The atomic swap can lose its entire second commit and D4 stays
   green. With `img.patch_entry(efi_entry_off, ...)` removed (plant A), the UEFI
   twin keeps pointing at the factory kernel while /limine.conf moves to the new
   release, yet every check passes and the harness prints PASS (section 3). No
   D4 scenario boots a completed install on either firmware, and no scenario
   compares the two config chains after an install. The suite's only OVMF boot
   is the split state, which is expected to boot the old release. A UEFI machine
   would run an old kernel forever and nothing here would say so.

F2. The boot oracle is release-blind. Every boot is graded on the single string
   NOVA_BOOT_OK, and the payload kernel is the built kernel with a few marker
   bytes appended that are never executed, so the old kernel and the new kernel
   are indistinguishable on the serial line. Even a boot of the post-commit
   state cannot show that the installed release ran. Grading on a version or
   sequence string printed by the new kernel would fix F1 and F2 together.

F3. Scenario 2 (USB removed mid-install) is not a QEMU test and does not model
   USB removal. No EIO, no short read, no failed write: the "USB" cases call the
   same os._exit fault hook as power loss, and the four TestUsbRemovalRecovery
   cases never start QEMU. The claim in docs/team.md that USB removal was shown
   "bootable under QEMU" rests on power-loss boots of the same states.

F4. A sweep that tests nothing reports success. When the state-list probe fails
   (section 5), the harness prints "0 states tested, 0 failures" and does not
   set FAILED. Nothing asserts the expected count of 15, so 15 boots can vanish
   silently. The harness should assert the expected count, as it already asserts
   exit codes.

F5 (low). Scenario 1's three boots are three boots of an unchanged image: the
   installer verifies the package before it opens the disk. That satisfies
   "fails closed" only in the weakest sense, and the harness checks only the
   installer's exit status, not that the refusal was a verification refusal (a
   Python traceback would also count as a pass).

F6 (low). boot_fail_expected() calls "no NOVA_BOOT_OK within 30 s" a pass, so a
   QEMU that never starts (bad binary path, missing firmware) also counts as
   "the mutant image refused to boot". The sabotage gate cannot tell a crash
   from an unbootable image; it should assert QEMU started and produced serial
   output.

VERDICT: FAIL
  Power loss at all 15 operation points is genuinely booted under QEMU, and a
  broken root-pointer ordering is caught (plant B). But the second half of the
  atomic swap -- the UEFI twin commit -- can be deleted with the whole suite
  still green and calling itself PASS (plant A), no scenario ever boots a
  completed install, the boot oracle cannot tell which release ran, USB removal
  is never simulated as a device event nor booted, and the sweep silently
  reports success when it tests zero states.
